  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA cold front will push in from the northwest late tonight into\nWednesday morning, stall/waiver over or just south of the area\nthrough Thursday, before finally getting flushed out well to \nthe south Thursday night. The main forecast challenges through \nThursday will be determining the timing/placement of various \nrain chances, along with if locally heavy rainfall can occur \nwith repeated rounds of showers/storms.\n\nA shortwave will zip across southern Ontario/northern NY this\nevening, bringing an initial uptick in shower potential,\nparticularly across the northern portions of the area (including\nRochester/Watertown/the Tug Hill). Another uptick in showers, \nwith potential for a bit of thunder, is expected into the \nSouthern Tier and Finger Lakes region late tonight/pre-dawn \nWednesday amid continued warm air/moisture advection ahead of \nthe approaching cold front. \n\nThe cold front will push across portions of the area through the\nday Wednesday before stalling closer to the PA boarder.\nLocations north of the front should be dry on Wednesday, with\npotential for scattered showers/an isolated storm continuing \nalong and south of the front. These rain chances should become \nlargely confined to the Southern Tier and Finger Lakes by \nWednesday afternoon. \n\nA couple of weak shortwaves are expected to cross the region \nWednesday night into Thursday, each bringing an increase in \nshower/storm chances with their passages. The front will likely \nstall by Wednesday night, lift back north slightly into Thursday,\nbefore finally sweeping southeast by Thursday night. Rain \nchances will focus near and just south of the front. Deep-layer \nflow will largely parallel to the front, which can support \ntraining and/or repeated rounds of rain/storms as the front. \nWhat becomes concerning is precipitable water values climbing to\n2.00-2.25\" along/south of the front Wednesday night into \nThursday morning, which is near or above record values for this \nlate in the year per KBUF sounding climatology. All of this to \nsay that we will need to monitor potential for heavy rain \nWednesday night into Thursday where repeated rounds or training \nrain/storms occur, most likely along and just south of the \nfront. There is disagreement regarding whether the front will \nremain closer to the PA border Wednesday night and Thursday, or \nif it will be able to lift to closer to I-90. Weak instability\nis expected to limit or preclude any severe wx potential. \n\nHigh temperatures in the mid to upper 70s with overnight lows\nranging from the upper 50s to mid 60s are expected over the next\ncouple of days. Dew points will climb well into the 60s and\nperhaps to near 70 at times for Wednesday and Thursday,\nespecially south of the front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nStrong high pressure briefly slides from Ontario/Quebec into New\nEngland on Friday, bringing a brief spell of much cooler and\ndrier weather to the local area. Friday night will be the\ncoolest night, with much of the area (outside of urban centers\nand the lakeshores) likely to dip into the 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe active frontal boundary is expected to lift northeast back\ntowards southern NY this weekend into early next week. There is\nquite a bit of disagreement regarding the placement of high\npressure over New England and this front. Some guidance suggests\nstronger high pressure remains, keeping the front farther\nsouthwest and suggesting a drier local forecast. Other guidance\ndepicts weaker high pressure, which would allow the front and\noccasional rain potential to lift farther northeast and\npotentially into Upstate NY this weekend into early next week.\nThe forecast is rather generic with several periods with chance\nto likely PoPs for rain Saturday through Tuesday, with the\nexpectations that we'll be able to better refine that period\nover the coming days.",
